  "answer_text": "## Root cause\n\nThe program spills the context pointer (`r1` = `ctx`) into the 8\u2011byte stack slot at `fp-8`:\n\n```\n*(u64*)(r10 - 8) = r1;     // fp-8 .. fp-1 now hold a spilled ctx pointer\n```\n\nThen it stores a single byte **inside that same 8\u2011byte slot**:\n\n```\nr0 = 0x23;\n*(u8*)(r10 - 7) = r0;      // writes into byte fp-7, i.e. part of the spilled pointer\n```\n\nA spilled register is only tracked as a pointer while its whole 8\u2011byte slot is left untouched. Overwriting any byte of it invalidates the spill, so the verifier downgrades `fp-8` to a scalar (`fp-8=mmmmmmmm`). When the value is read back:\n\n```\nr0 = *(u64*)(r10 - 8);     // R0 = scalar(), not ctx\nr0 = *(u64*)(r0 + 8);      // dereference of a scalar -> rejected\n```\n\nHence:\n\n```\nR0 invalid mem access 'scalar'\n```\n\nThis is exactly the verifier\u2019s intended behavior: a partial write to a spilled pointer kills the pointer information.\n\n## Fix\n\nTo make the program verify, don\u2019t clobber the spilled pointer \u2014 put the byte store in a stack slot that does **not** overlap `fp-8 .. fp-1` (for example `fp-16`). That keeps `fp-8` a valid `ctx` spill, so the reload yields a pointer again.\n\nNote that the final dereference also has to be a *legal* context access. A `u64` read at `ctx + 8` is not a valid `__sk_buff` access, so change it to a valid field/size (e.g. read `len` as `u32` at offset 0).\n\n```c\nSEC(\"socket\")\n__naked void entry(void)\n{\n\tasm volatile (\"\t\t\t\t\t\\\n\t*(u64*)(r10 - 8) = r1;\t\t\t\t\\\n\tr0 = 0x23;\t\t\t\t\t\\\n\t*(u8*)(r10 - 16) = r0;\t\t\t\t\\\n\tr0 = *(u64*)(r10 - 8);\t\t\t\t\\\n\tr0 = *(u32*)(r0 + 0);\t\t\t\t\\\n\texit;\t\t\t\t\t\t\\\n\"\t::: __clobber_all);\n}\n```\n\n### Why this passes\n- `*(u8*)(r10 - 16)` writes to a separate stack slot, leaving the 8\u2011byte spill at `fp-8` intact, so `r0 = *(u64*)(r10 - 8)` reloads a proper `ctx()` pointer instead of a scalar \u2014 eliminating the \u201cinvalid mem access 'scalar'\u201d error.\n- Reading `*(u32*)(r0 + 0)` is a valid `__sk_buff` context access, so the subsequent dereference is accepted.\n\nThe essential, minimal change is the first one (move the byte store off `fp-7` so it no longer overlaps the spilled pointer); the dereference adjustment is needed only because the original `u64` ctx read at offset 8 would not be a legal context access once the pointer is preserved.",
